\description{
Builds a \code{compD} object from a set of composition parts. It validates the
parts (zeroes permitted), summarizes zero occurrence, imputes any zeroes,
optionally flags compositional outliers and stores the closed composition.
}
\details{
Compositional data carry only relative information, and a zero has no
log-ratio. When zeroes are present, \code{\link[=comp_impute]{comp_impute()}} imputes them on the
original (pre-closure) scale before \code{\link[compositions:acomp]{compositions::acomp()}} closes the
result. Every downstream description, plot and coordinate is computed from
this imputed, closed composition. The zero-free values on the original scale
keep each row's absolute total and are stored as \code{parts_imputed} for the
\code{include_total} T-space covariate of \code{\link[=comp_iv]{comp_iv()}}. Without zeroes, no
imputation is performed and \code{parts_imputed} equals the input parts.
}
\section{Outlier flagging}{

With \code{detect_outliers = TRUE}, \code{\link[=comp_outliers]{comp_outliers()}} runs on the zero-free parts
after imputation, and its per-observation columns are appended to \code{data_raw}
as \code{.outlier_distance}, \code{.outlier_limit} and \code{.outlier}. Rows are flagged and
never dropped. Every field of the object keeps one entry per input
observation, and \code{data_raw}, \code{composition} and \code{parts_imputed} stay
row-aligned with the input and with each other.

Filtering is left to the caller. Dropping the flagged rows means declaring
the composition again, which recomputes it (and \code{closure = "mean"}) on the
remaining rows. The \code{.outlier} columns must be dropped in the same step.
\code{declare_composition()} writes them itself and stops with an error on any
input that already carries them, whether or not detection is requested.

